haha,
with a solution like this... of course you are forgiven!
I will try it imediately when I come home ...

In response to Professor Johnson, I would like to emphasize I only
picked up R recently. ANd I am not an engineer nor programmer (just a
humble biologist)...

Reading the forums and news on this topic I was just hoping that the
plyr package would one day work with parallel computing.
The reason I expected it to work was the following:

We have been using the snow package for parallel computing and that
linearly reduces the calculation time with the number of cores,  as
shown in the example below.

load('dataset.rdata')

aSplitListFactor <- list(dataset$colSeq,dataset$rowSeq)

system.time(sapply(split(dataset,aSplitListFactor),function(...)
Sys.sleep(0.05)))

user  system elapsed
 0.47    0.00   63.45

## Now using snow

library(snow)

cl <-makeSOCKcluster(c("localhost","localhost"))
clusterEvalQ(cl, library(drc))

system.time(parSapply(cl,split(dataset,aSplitListFactor),

function(...) Sys.sleep(0.05)))
 user  system elapsed

   0.50    0.00   32.14

stopCluster(cl)

## unfortunately I could not get snow to work together with plyr...
however I find plyr much easier to work with.. and I am now pleased to
see that the waiting is rewarded!

Thanks everybody!
Rene

